        "id": "ch3_2_1-2",
        "text": "The second way we can use Select-Object is to select a subset of objects from a collection. The\nparameters for doing that are in the first parameter set; -first , -last , and -skip . Each of these\nparameters requires an integer as an argument. -first 5 will select the first five objects in the\npipeline, whereas -last 2 will select the last two objects in the pipeline, like this:\nFigure 3.5 \u2013 Selecting a subset of objects\nWe can use the -skip parameter to miss out values at the start or end, like this:\n1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 | Select-Object -First 2 -skip 1\nThis will return the integers 2 and 3 which are the first two if we skip the first one.\nActivity 1\nHow might we return 2 , 3 , 4 , 7 , and 8 from that array?\nThe problem with running Select-Object in this way is that unless we can control the order\nof the objects in the collection, we\u2019re just grabbing random objects. This brings us to our next\ncmdlet, Sort-Object .",
